# Use writes of `held`: a text or a list. The loop reads the variable anew in each turn and holds the element against a text of one unit, so the front reads the variable once and tells a text from a list.
# In the copy for each the reads of the variable are expected what the copy is built for. Without that the text's copy guessed a list at the read, failed in every turn, and never came to know that its
# turns run no other code, which is what lets the value read in front stand for the variable.
# f1 = count
# RUN: walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# CHECK: = TypeTest {{*}} testedType=string
# CHECK: = GetStringCodeUnit
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell globalThis.sink = () => {};
# ANSWERS: 3 3 2
